== Solution 1==
The circumference of a circle is obtained by simply multiplying the radius by <imath>2\pi</imath>. So, the C-coordinate (in this case, it is the x-coordinate) will increase at a steady rate. The area, however, is obtained by squaring the radius and multiplying it by <imath>\pi</imath>. Since squares do not increase in an evenly spaced arithmetic sequence, the increase in the A-coordinates (aka the y- coordinates) will be much more significant. The answer is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(A)}}, 
</imath><asy>
size(75);
pair A= (1.5,2) ,
B= (3,4) ,
C= (4.5,7) ,
D= (6,11) ,
E= (7.5,16) ;
draw((0,-1)--(0,16));
draw((-1,0)--(16,0));
dot(A^^B^^C^^D^^E);
label("$A$", (0,8), W);
label("$C$", (8,0), S);</asy>.
-RBANDA
== Solution 2 (parametric plot) == 
Since we know that the formula for circumference of a circle is <imath>2\pi r</imath>, and for the area of a circle it is <imath>\pi r^2</imath>, any coordinate is simply <imath>(2\pi r, \pi r^2)</imath>, notice that, in more similar terms, it is <imath>(2\pi t, \pi t^2)</imath>, which is a parametric plot. So let:
<cmath>X(t)=2\pi t</cmath>
<cmath>Y(t) = \pi t^2</cmath>
Solving for <imath>t</imath> gives <imath>\frac{X(t)}{2\pi} = t</imath>, thus letting <imath>Y(t)=y</imath> and <imath>X(t)=x</imath>,
<cmath>y=\pi (\frac{x^2}{4\pi^2})</cmath>
<cmath>y=\frac{x^2}{4\pi}</cmath>
Since the domain of the function is <imath>x \geq 0</imath>, note that the function is always increasing quadratically. The only answer choice that fits this function is <imath>\boxed{(A)}</imath>

